  "circulationNotes": "The primary circulation axis on the Ground Floor moves from the East-facing verandah through the foyer, providing access to the formal living, dining, and the central staircase. The L-shaped massing defines a clear pathway, while the internal courtyard acts as a visual and airflow focal point, enhancing semi-private zone connectivity. On the First Floor, the landing serves as a central hub, efficiently distributing access to all three bedrooms, the common bath, the study, and the family lounge, maintaining privacy for sleeping zones.",
  "rationale": "The design for this 40'x60' plot in Kochi meticulously balances client requirements with site opportunities and climate challenges, rooted in Kerala Traditional architecture. The 'L-shaped' building footprint, strategically placed within the setbacks (4' side, 8' front, 5' rear), maximizes the buildable area while creating a sheltered internal 'courtyard' (nadumuttam). This courtyard is central to the climate-adaptive strategy, promoting stack effect ventilation and bringing diffused light deep into the core, aligning with the hot-humid climate. The East-facing orientation is capitalized with a spacious verandah and large living room windows, welcoming morning light and breezes. The ground floor caters to public and semi-private functions with a well-defined sequence from verandah-foyer-living-dining. The master bedroom on the ground floor ensures accessibility and flexibility. Service areas (kitchen, utility, common bath) are efficiently consolidated on the NW side, minimizing disturbance to primary living zones and optimizing plumbing runs (budget-first principle). The dog-legged staircase, centrally located, provides clear vertical circulation. The first floor dedicates space to two secondary bedrooms, a common bath (stacked for efficiency), a small study, and a family lounge, creating a private retreat. The balcony offers an outdoor extension for the upper floor. The overall plan ensures 3 bedrooms, 3 bathrooms, and 1 car parking, while respecting the moderate budget through efficient massing and wet-zone consolidation, all while embodying the distinctive aesthetic and functional wisdom of Kerala Traditional architecture.",
  "modeConsiderations": [
    {
      "mode": "climate_adaptive",
      "note": "The internal courtyard acts as a thermal buffer and lightwell, drawing hot air upwards and facilitating cross-ventilation. Shaded verandahs on the East provide passive cooling. Minimized West openings and strategic window placements (East/North) reduce solar heat gain while maximizing natural light and airflow, critical for the Kochi climate. Pitched roof design (conceptual) would further aid in heat dissipation."
    }
  ],
